```cpp
template<typename T> std::vector<T> MakeVec(size_t len)
{
  std::vector<T> vec_out(len);
  return vec_out;
}

void Foo()
{
  std::vector<int> vec = MakeVec<int>(10);
}
```
Normally `MakeVec` would be called, and then `vec_out` would be generated, and then `vec` would be created, and then finally `vec_out` would be copied over to `vec` and destroyed. Copy elision is simply when the compiler detects that `vec` can be used immediately without having to create `vec_out`.

